<% content_for :title, "Chart — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, t("components.pages.chart.subtitle") %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <header class="space-y-3">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em] leading-[0.92] sm:text-5xl">Chart</h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-snug text-muted-foreground"><%= t("components.pages.chart.subtitle") %></p>
  </header>

  <%= ui_card(class: "p-6", data: { docs_toc_skip: "" }) do %>
    <%= render "components/examples/chart_area" %>
  <% end %>

  <%= render "components/install", name: "chart" %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:importmap], language: :ruby) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">How a chart is put together</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      A chart is a container and the parts you put in it. The container, <%= docs_code("ui_chart") %>,
      holds what every part shares: the rows, the config and the layout. Each part draws one layer of
      the chart — <%= docs_code("ui_chart_grid") %>, <%= docs_code("ui_chart_x_axis") %>,
      <%= docs_code("ui_chart_bar") %>, <%= docs_code("ui_chart_tooltip") %> — and the parts stack in
      the order you write them, like the children of a Recharts chart. There is no chart type to pick:
      bars and a line in one container make a composed chart, and plain markup inside it sits on top
      of the drawing.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The drawing is D3's. The container's Stimulus controller, <%= docs_code("ui-chart") %>, measures
      the chart, works out the scales and the stacks, and hands them to every part in a
      <%= docs_code("ui-chart:render") %> event. Each part has a controller of its own that draws its
      own <%= docs_code("<svg>") %> with D3, so a part you do not use is a file you can delete — and a
      part you need is one you can write. The colors are CSS custom properties: switching the theme
      repaints the chart without redrawing it. D3 itself is imported only on the pages that have a chart.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Your first chart</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Let's build a bar chart, then add a grid, an axis, a tooltip and a legend, one part at a time.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Start with the rows</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      One hash per category, in any shape: <%= docs_code("data_key:") %> names the field each part
      reads. Rows usually come from a query. Dates and times serialize as ISO strings, which the axes
      and the tooltip format; a <%= docs_code("BigDecimal") %> arrives as a string and is read as a number.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:rows], language: :ruby) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Define the config</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The config holds what the rows do not: each series' label and color. It is kept apart from the
      data on purpose, so several charts can share one config — and the rows can come from anywhere.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:config], language: :ruby) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Compose the chart</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      One <%= docs_code("ui_chart_bar") %> per series. The container is
      <%= docs_code("aspect-video") %> by default; give it a <%= docs_code("min-h-*") %> or a height
      too, or it has no room to draw in.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:bars]) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Add a grid</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("ui_chart_grid") %> draws a line at each tick of the value axis.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:grid]) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Add an axis</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The x axis labels the categories with the <%= docs_code("data_key:") %> field.
      <%= docs_code("tick_format:") %> is a d3-time-format specifier here — the months are dates — so
      the labels read <em>Jan</em>, <em>Feb</em>… in the language of the page.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:axis]) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Add a tooltip</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Point at a month and <%= docs_code("ui_chart_tooltip") %> lists each series' value there, under
      the month's full name.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:tooltip]) %>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.006em]">Add a legend</h3>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("ui_chart_legend") %> names the series with the config's labels and takes the room
      it needs below the plot.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:legend]) %>

    <%= render "components/example", **@first_chart %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]"><%= t("components.sections.composition") %></h2>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:composition], language: :text) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Chart config</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Each key of the config is a series — or a category that a pie, a ring or a legend names. It takes
      a <%= docs_code("label:") %>, a <%= docs_code("color:") %> or a <%= docs_code("theme:") %> with one
      color per theme, and a Lucide <%= docs_code("icon:") %>, which the legend and the tooltip show in
      place of the color swatch.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:config_full], language: :ruby) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Theming</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Each color in the config becomes a <%= docs_code("--color-<key>") %> custom property scoped to the
      chart, and every part paints with those. Point them at the theme's chart tokens and the chart
      follows the theme, dark mode included.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:theme_tokens], language: :css) %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Any CSS color works as well — hex, <%= docs_code("hsl()") %>, <%= docs_code("oklch()") %> — and
      the variables reach past the parts: a part's own <%= docs_code("color:") %>, a row's
      <%= docs_code("fill") %> and your own markup inside the chart can all use them.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:colors_elsewhere]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Tooltip</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The tooltip shows a label — the category — and a row per series with an indicator, a name and a
      value. <%= docs_code("indicator:") %> is <%= docs_code(":dot") %>, <%= docs_code(":line") %> or
      <%= docs_code(":dashed") %>; <%= docs_code("hide_label:") %> and <%= docs_code("hide_indicator:") %>
      leave either out. <%= docs_code("cursor:") %> shades the category the pointer is on, and
      <%= docs_code("default_index:") %> opens the tooltip before anyone points.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("label_key:") %> and <%= docs_code("name_key:") %> read the label and the names from
      another config entry or field. Here the label is <em>Total visitors</em> and the names come from
      the browser each slice belongs to:
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:tooltip_keys]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Legend</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The legend lists the series — or, for a pie, its slices — with the config's labels and colors.
      <%= docs_code("name_key:") %> lists the rows instead, named by a field, and
      <%= docs_code("vertical_align: :top") %> moves it above the plot.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:legend_name_key]) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Formatting</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The axes' <%= docs_code("tick_format:") %>, the tooltip's <%= docs_code("label_format:") %> and
      <%= docs_code("value_format:") %> and the label lists' <%= docs_code("format:") %> are D3
      specifiers: d3-format for numbers, d3-time-format for dates. Without one, numbers are written the
      way the page's language writes them.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:formats]) %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The month and day names and the separators come from the app's own translations —
      <%= docs_code("date.month_names") %>, <%= docs_code("date.abbr_day_names") %>,
      <%= docs_code("number.format.delimiter") %> and the rest of Rails' keys — so an app that speaks
      Portuguese gets <em>Fev</em> and <em>1.234,5</em> with no setup on the chart.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">A layer of your own</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The built-in parts are no different from one you write. <%= docs_code("ui_chart_layer") %> puts an
      <%= docs_code("<svg>") %> over the chart and wires it to a Stimulus controller of yours, which
      receives the chart's scales and draws with D3. This one draws a goal line:
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:layer]) %>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:layer_controller], language: :javascript) %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      A part hears three events, in this order. <%= docs_code("event.detail.chart") %> carries the rows,
      the config, the plot's box, the category and value scales, the polar geometry, a formatter and
      <%= docs_code("d3") %> itself.
    </p>
    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:events], language: :text) %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]">Accessibility</h2>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      <%= docs_code("label:") %> names the chart. The drawing is hidden from assistive technology; the
      legend is text. With a tooltip, the chart takes focus: the arrow keys, <kbd>Home</kbd> and
      <kbd>End</kbd> move from category to category, <kbd>Escape</kbd> closes it, and what the tooltip
      shows is announced. With reduced motion set, nothing animates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-8">
    <h2 class="border-b pb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-[-0.01em]"><%= t("components.sections.examples") %></h2>
    <% @examples.each do |example| %>
      <%= render "components/example", **example %>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <%= render "components/api", name: "chart" %>
</article>
